<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  office2.world: a full 24 x 18 m office-building floor plan for the Husky
  VSLAM sim, built from a supplied architectural floor plan (transcribed by
  the user; no image file exists in-repo, see docs/superpowers/specs for the
  design note). Same offline-only discipline as office.world: shapes + the
  Gazebo built-in `gazebo.material` script library only, no fuel.gazebosim.org
  downloads, no external meshes.

  Floor plan (top-down, +X east, +Y north), 24 x 18 m overall, origin at the
  building's geometric centre:

   Y=9  +==========+========+========+========+==========+==========+
        | Pantry   |Office 1|Office 2|Meeting |Office 3  | Server/  |
        | /Kitchen |        |        |  Room  |          | IT Room  |
   Y=5.5+==========+========+========+========+==========+==========+
        | Male     |                                     | Storage  |
        | Toilet   |     corridor (north leg, 2.5 m)      |          |
   Y=2.5+==========+=============================+==========+==========+
        | Female   |                            |         | Copy /   |
        | Toilet   |                            |         | Print    |
   Y=0  +==========+  Open Office A | Open      +=========+==========+
        :  (open   :    (6x6)       | Office B  :  (open  :
   Y=-4 +  lobby   +================+  (6x6)    +  lobby  +
        :  pocket  |  Reception |Office 4|Office 5|  pocket :
   Y=-7 +==========+============+========+========+=========+
        |      entrance / reception foyer (2.0 m, closed loop)      |
   Y=-9 +======================[ENTRANCE]============================+
       X=-12                          X=0                          X=12

  ===========================================================================
  WHY THIS IS A CLOSED-LOOP CORRIDOR, NOT A TREE
  ===========================================================================
  The source plan labels the corridor "CORRIDOR (CLOSED LOOP)": a north leg
  (2.5 m) collects the four north-row rooms, then two side branches run SOUTH
  AROUND the two 6x6 open-plan rooms (not between them; Open Office A and B
  share a common centre wall at x=0), down to a 2.0 m entrance/reception foyer
  that ties both branches back together. That foyer is the loop closure: you
  can walk north leg, west branch, foyer, east branch, back to the north leg
  and never retrace a corridor segment, which is exactly the kind of geometry
  RTAB-Map loop closure needs (see office.world's own corridor-loop rationale
  and ENGINEERING_NOTES.md's sidewalk-world loop-closure notes).

  ===========================================================================
  TEXTURE DISCIPLINE, copied verbatim from office.world, not re-derived
  ===========================================================================
  office.world already paid for this lesson: an all-different-flat-colour
  wall set measured 13 ORB keypoints in a 1920x1200 frame (RTAB-Map needs 20
  inliers just to compute one pose) until every wall got a real IMAGE texture
  instead of a flat paint colour. Every wall segment below uses one of the
  ~20 textured (non-flat, non-glow, non-transparent) materials in this
  Gazebo install's own material script, cycled so touching/facing segments
  never repeat: textured for odometry, distinct for loop closure, per
  ENGINEERING_NOTES.md Sec 2.6/2.12. Large surfaces (the two 6x6 open-plan rooms, the
  corridor run) are tiled in a grid rather than one stretched slab, same as
  office.world's 4x4 floor grid: Gazebo paints one texture copy per box
  face, so a single 6 m slab would smear one copy across the whole room.

  ===========================================================================
  DIMENSIONAL NOTE, flagged, not silently guessed
  ===========================================================================
  The source brief lists Server/IT (3.0x3.5), Storage (3.0x3.0), Copy/Print
  (3.0x2.5) and Reception/Office4/Office5 (4.0-4.5 m deep) but also requires
  an exact 24 x 18 m footprint, an exact 6x6 open-plan pair, an exact 2.5 m
  north corridor, AND at least 2.0 m robot clearance at the entrance foyer.
  Summing every room at its literal spec depth overshoots 18 m by about 1 m.
  Resolved by: (a) widening the east utility stack (Server/IT, Storage,
  Copy/Print) from 3.0 to 3.5 m to close a grid gap next to Office 3, and
  (b) compressing the south-row rooms (Reception/Office4/Office5) from their
  assumed 4.0 m depth to 3.0 m to preserve the entrance foyer at the robot's
  stated MINIMUM clearance (2.0 m) rather than shrinking the explicitly
  stated 2.5 m north corridor or the exact 6x6 open-plan rooms. Net effect:
  total modelled room area is 247.0 sqm against the brief's 252.5 sqm target
  (1.8% low), close enough to trust the overall layout. See the session
  report for the full list of these flagged adjustments.

  DOOR WIDTH, revised 1.00 m -> 1.80 m after live testing found it too tight
  =========================================================================
  The brief states door clear width as 1.00 m, but also separately asks for
  2.0-2.5 m robot clearance "at every corridor/doorway" - a real conflict
  for doorways specifically. The first build of this file took the literal
  1.00 m door figure. Checking sidewalk_navigation/config/costmap_common.yaml
  and README.md against that choice: the Husky's documented footprint is
  0.99 x 0.67 m, circumscribed radius 0.598 m, i.e. it sweeps a 1.196 m
  diameter circle just turning in place, already wider than a 1.00 m door
  before the robot ever approaches at an angle. README.md itself flags this
  footprint number as `measured: false`, an unverified placeholder, so
  1.196 m is a floor, not a target.

  Every interior and exterior doorway below is now 1.80 m clear width: about
  50% margin over the 1.196 m sweep circle (comfortable headroom given the
  footprint is unmeasured and could be larger), while staying visibly
  narrower than the corridor it opens onto (2.0-2.5 m everywhere) so it still
  reads as a doorway rather than an open wall gap at this 24x18 m scale.
  Door centres were kept the same as the 1.00 m version wherever a room was
  large enough to absorb the extra 0.8 m symmetrically; three doors in the
  smaller utility rooms (Male Toilet/Storage's corridor door, and
  Copy/Print's) were re-centred on their own room's midpoint instead, to
  avoid leaving a near-zero sliver of wall at one end of an asymmetric room.
  Verified live: the robot was driven at both perpendicular and diagonal
  approach angles through doors of three different corridor/room geometries
  and the exterior main entrance, with get_model_state pose readback after
  each pass confirming it crossed cleanly with no collision-ejection tipping.
  See the session report for the exact per-door pass/fail results.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="office2">
    <include><uri>model://sun</uri></include>
    <include><uri>model://ground_plane</uri></include>

    <!-- ==== textured floor skin ==========================================
         VISUAL ONLY, same convention as office.world: no <collision> here,
         physics floor stays the ground_plane above. Tiled per room (and the
         corridor/foyer) so no single texture copy stretches across more than
         ~3.3 m of surface. Corridor + foyer + both branch pockets share one
         dedicated material (Gazebo/Footway) so the plan's own
         "CORRIDOR / WALKWAY" floor legend reads as a visually distinct zone
         from every room floor. -->
    <model name="floor_skin">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="tiles">
        <visual name="f001"><pose>-10.25 7.25 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.5 3.5 0.004</size></box></geometry>
    <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodFloor</name></script></material></visual>
