    <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Footway</name></script></material></visual>

    <!-- ==== overhead presentation camera ===============================
         WHAT THIS IS. One downward-looking colour camera, attached to
         nothing, hanging 26 m over the centre of the building and publishing
         a plain image on /overhead_cam/image_raw - the same topic name
         office.world uses, which is safe because only one world is ever
         loaded at a time. Point a recorder at that topic and every mapping
         run of this world produces a top-down video of the robot driving the
         floor plan, at no extra effort during the run. There is no ceiling
         in this world - only walls and a floor skin - so a camera above it
         looks straight down into every room, corridor branch and the
         entrance foyer.

         WHY IT LIVES INSIDE THE WORLD INSTEAD OF BEING A SCREEN RECORDER.
         Measured runs are launched with the graphical client switched off
         (gui:=false) and no windows on screen, which is most of why they run
         roughly four times faster than a watched run. There is consequently
         nothing on screen for a screen recorder to capture. The simulation
         server, however, renders camera sensors inside its own process
         whether or not any window exists - that is the confirmed rule in
         ENGINEERING_NOTES.md section 5 - so a camera placed in the world yields a real
         rendered picture at the full headless speed. The top-down picture at
         logs/sidewalk_evaluation/results/phase1/fix_verify/gazebo_birdseye.png
         was produced exactly this way with a temporary camera that was never
         kept. This is that same technique, made permanent.

         THE CAVEAT THAT WILL CATCH SOMEBODY OUT. Rendering any camera at all
         needs a graphics context, and the simulation server takes that from
         the DISPLAY and XAUTHORITY environment variables. Launch over a
         remote shell without both of them exported and this camera is never
         created: no images, no camera information, and no error that names
         the cause - just the line "Unable to create CameraSensor. Rendering
         is disabled." in the server log, after which everything else carries
         on normally. The robot's own stereo camera has exactly the same
         requirement, so in practice a run that produced no overhead video is
         a run that produced no camera images at all, and the fix is the same
         one: export both variables before launching.

         HOW THE 26 m HEIGHT WAS COMPUTED. It was derived for THIS building,
         not copied from office.world - that floor plan is 14 by 12 m and its
         camera hangs at 18 m. The method is the same; only the footprint
         changes.

         The lens is set to a 1.047 radian (60.0 degree) horizontal field of
         view, matching office.world so the two videos look like the same
         instrument. The simulator derives the vertical field of view from
         the image shape, so an 800 by 600 picture covers a patch of floor
         with the same 4 : 3 proportions as the picture itself:

             half-width  a = height * tan(field of view / 2) = height * 0.5772
             half-height b = a * 600 / 800                   = a * 0.75

         The building is 24 m east-west by 18 m north-south, so its floor
         half-extents are 12 m and 9 m. That is not the whole requirement,
         because the walls are 2.5 m tall and this is a perspective lens, not
         an overhead orthographic drawing. Seen from a point directly above
         the centre, the TOP outer edge of a wall standing at horizontal
         distance d appears to lie at

             d * height / (height - 2.5)

         so the frame has to cover more than the floor footprint or the tops
         of the outer walls are sliced off at the edges. Requiring a further
         10 % margin beyond even that:

             east-west   0.5772 * (height - 2.5) >= 12 * 1.10 -> height >= 25.37 m
             north-south 0.4329 * (height - 2.5) >= 9  * 1.10 -> height >= 25.37 m

         Both constraints land on the same number here, unlike office.world,
         because this building's 24 : 18 footprint is exactly the picture's
         4 : 3 - the frame runs out of room in both directions at once.
         Rounded up to a flat 26 m, which delivers:

             ground coverage        30.02 m east-west by 22.51 m north-south
             margin past wall tops  13.0 % in both directions
             clear border at floor  3.01 m beyond the east and west walls,
                                    2.26 m beyond the north and south walls,
                                    which also keeps the entrance plaza and
                                    its planters (out to y = -9.85) in frame
             scale                  37.5 mm of floor per pixel, so the Husky
                                    (about 1.0 by 0.67 m) is roughly 27 by 18
                                    pixels - smaller than in office.world
                                    because the building is larger, but still
                                    plainly a robot moving between rooms,
                                    which is all this clip has to show.

         WHICH WAY IS UP IN THE PICTURE. A camera here looks along its own
         forward axis with its own upward axis at the top of the frame.
         Pitching it a quarter turn (1.5708 radians) nose-down aims it at the
         floor; the further quarter turn of yaw then rolls the frame so that
         north (+Y) is at the top and east (+X) is at the right. The video
         therefore has the same orientation as the floor-plan sketch at the
         top of this file, so the two can be held side by side while
         explaining a run - which matters more here than in office.world,
         because the closed corridor loop is hard to describe in words.

         WHY THIS CANNOT DISTURB A MAPPING RUN.
           * It is not attached to the robot. It is its own static model at a
             fixed pose, contributing no transform, no joint and no mass to
             the robot.
           * Its link carries no collision shape and no visual shape at all,
             only the sensor. The robot cannot drive into it, the navigation
             costmap never sees it, and the robot's own stereo camera cannot
             see it even looking straight up.
           * It publishes on /overhead_cam/image_raw and
             /overhead_cam/camera_info, and nothing in the mapping stack
             subscribes to either. sidewalk_slam/launch/rtabmap_common.launch
             was read to confirm this: it subscribes only to the colour,
             depth, left, right, camera-information, inertial and odometry
             topics beneath the robot camera's own namespace (zedx_front /
             zed_node by default), plus its own internal bundled image topic.
           * It costs roughly 7 % of what the robot's stereo pair already
             costs: 800 by 600 at 10 frames per simulated second is 4.8
             million pixels per second and 14.4 megabytes per second of image
             traffic, against 69 million pixels per second and 207 megabytes
             per second for two 1920 by 1200 colour lenses at 15 hertz. That
             ratio is the point - this machine is already carrying physics,
             the stereo camera and the mapping software at once, and
             over-subscribing it has caused real failures before (see
             docs/OPERATIONS.md on the memory and queue-depth incident).

         WHY 800 BY 600 AT 10 FRAMES PER SECOND. This picture is watched, not
         measured: no pose is ever computed from it, so the only requirement
         is that a person can see where the robot is. 10 frames of SIMULATED
         time per second, played back at 10 frames per second, shows the
         motion at true speed no matter what multiple of real time the
         simulation happened to be running at. Raising either number buys
         nothing and takes graphics time away from the stereo camera, which
         is the one that has to keep up. Note this is the SAME resolution as
         office.world even though this building is larger: the extra pixels
         would go on empty floor, and the cost is what has to stay small.
    -->
    <model name="overhead_cam">
      <static>true</static>
      <!-- 26 m above the building centre. Pitch 1.5708 aims the lens straight
           down; the matching yaw puts north at the top of the frame. -->
      <pose>0 0 26.0 0 1.5708 1.5708</pose>
      <link name="lens">
        <sensor name="overhead_camera" type="camera">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>10</update_rate>
          <!-- No frustum drawn in the graphical client: it would sit across
               the middle of the scene during hand-driving sessions. -->
          <visualize>false</visualize>
          <camera name="overhead">
            <horizontal_fov>1.047</horizontal_fov>
            <image><width>800</width><height>600</height><format>R8G8B8</format></image>
            <!-- Clipping is measured along the lens axis, and the floor is
                 exactly 26 m along it, so the far plane only has to clear 26.
                 50 m leaves room to raise the camera later without silently
                 clipping the floor away. -->
            <clip><near>1.0</near><far>50.0</far></clip>
            <!-- Deliberately NO <noise> here. The robot's stereo camera
                 carries Gaussian noise on purpose, because its covariance has
                 to be honest (ENGINEERING_NOTES.md section 2.3). This one is only ever
                 looked at, and grain would just make the video harder to
                 read. -->
          </camera>
          <plugin name="overhead_cam_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_camera.so">
            <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
            <!-- 0.0 means "publish whenever the sensor renders", so the
                 10 hertz above is the single place the rate is set. -->
            <updateRate>0.0</updateRate>
            <!-- Camera name plus image topic name is what forms the published
                 topic: /overhead_cam/image_raw, with /overhead_cam/camera_info
                 beside it. -->
            <cameraName>overhead_cam</cameraName>
            <imageTopicName>image_raw</imageTopicName>
            <cameraInfoTopicName>camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
            <!-- Nothing publishes a transform for this frame and nothing
                 needs one: the picture is watched in an image viewer or
                 written to a video file, never projected into the map. -->
            <frameName>overhead_cam_optical_frame</frameName>
            <distortionK1>0.0</distortionK1>
            <distortionK2>0.0</distortionK2>
            <distortionK3>0.0</distortionK3>
            <distortionT1>0.0</distortionT1>
            <distortionT2>0.0</distortionT2>
          </plugin>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

    <physics type="ode"><real_time_update_rate>1000</real_time_update_rate><max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size></physics>
    <scene><ambient>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</ambient><background>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</background><shadows>false</shadows></scene>
  </world>
</sdf>
